SUMMARY:Applied Mathematics Seminar: Aina G. Irbe — Towards Inclusive Int
 elligence: Defining the Research Challenges at the Intersection of Artific
 ial Intelligence\, Universal Design for Learning\, and Workplace Training
LOCATION:GP 208\, The University of Alabama\, Tuscaloosa\, Alabama
DESCRIPTION:Aina G. Irbe\, Chief Operating Officer\, Accessible Minds\nTowa
 rds Inclusive Intelligence: Defining the Research Challenges at the Inters
 ection of Artificial Intelligence\, Universal Design for Learning\, and Wo
 rkplace Training\nEarlier time and different room: 10:00 AM in GP 208.\nOr
 ganizations are increasingly using artificial intelligence (AI) to create 
 workplace and academic training. AI can generate content quickly\, adapt m
 aterials to different learners\, and deliver information in text\, audio\,
  and video. These features seem to align with Universal Design for Learnin
 g (UDL)\, a framework that supports diverse learners by providing multiple
  means of engagement\, representation\, and action and expression. However
 \, being efficient and adaptive is not the same as being inclusive. AI-gen
 erated training can still overlook barriers related to disability\, access
 ibility\, language\, cognition\, and knowledge transfer.\n\nThis raises th
 ree challenges in using AI to develop workplace and academic training: AI 
 is often optimized for goals that don't reflect inclusive learning\, it ca
 n introduce new accessibility barriers or amplify existing ones\, and ther
 e is no practical\, validated way to evaluate training developed with AI. 
 A proposed six-dimension framework based on UDL and the Web Content Access
 ibility Guidelines (WCAG) offers a way to assess whether AI-developed trai
 ning is accessible\, inclusive\, and educationally sound. Three research q
 uestions and recommendations for future studies outline the next steps nee
 ded to validate it.\nhttps://jiazhaomath.github.io/seminar/2026-2027.html#
 2026-10-16
